{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Shoham stones and setting stones, for the ephod and for the breastpiece.</b> The measure of the verse is: shoham stones for the ephod, and setting stones (אבני מלואים) for the breastpiece. The shoham stones were two; the setting stones were twelve. And the Ramban, z\"l, explained that the reason for מלואים is that they were whole (שלמות), and so Onkelos translated אשלמותא — that is, that they should not be stones hewn from a large quarry, lacking anything of themselves, for the power of a stone, its wholeness, and its special property exist only when it is whole and as it was created at the inception of its formation. Therefore it says of the stones of the breastpiece: ״ומלאת בו מלואת אבן״ — 'and you shall fill in it a setting of stone' — and it is written: ״יהיו במלואותם״ — 'they shall be in their settings' — but this was not said of the stones of the ephod, for the stones of the ephod were two, and it was possible that the engraving of the names of the tribes upon them was traced and incised as the impression of a seal, because it says of them: ״פתוחי חותם תפתח״ — 'you shall engrave them as seal engravings' — and it says of them: ״מעשה חרש אבן פתוחי חותם״ — 'the work of a stone engraver, seal engravings.' But concerning the stones of the breastpiece, which were twelve, it does not say 'the work of a stone engraver' (מעשה חרש), though it does mention 'seal engravings' (פתוחי חותם) and commands concerning them: ״יהיו במלואותם״ — 'they shall be in their settings.' Therefore Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, needed to use the shamir with them, for how is it possible that the stones of the breastpiece should be seal engravings yet remain in their fullness without loss? This is impossible except by means of the shamir. And so the Sages, z\"l, expounded in tractate Sotah at the end of the last chapter: 'These stones were not written with ink, as it says: ״פתוחי חותם״, and one does not separate them with a chisel, as it says: ״במלואותם״; rather, one writes upon them with ink and shows the shamir to them, and they split open of themselves like this fig that splits open in the days of heat and lacks nothing, and like this cleft that splits open in the rainy season and lacks nothing' — end of quotation." "<b>And you should know</b> that the totality of the items whose donation the verse mentions in this parashah is fifteen (ט\"ו), for the spices (בשמים) and the aromatics (סמים) are one thing. Corresponding to them we find in the verse of Genesis fifteen puncta (נקוד) — meaning, the vowel-points (הנקודות) excluding the shva quiescens (שואי\"ן) and the hateph segol (חטף סגו\"ל) — for the Tabernacle is a counterpart to the creation of the world, and the reason for this, both in the creation of the world and in the Tabernacle, is to allude to what is written (Isaiah 26:4): ״כי ביה ה' צור עולמים״. Corresponding to them, too, fifteen occurrences of ת\"ק (500) are found in the seven heavens: each heaven is ת\"ק, and between each heaven and the next is ת\"ק — that makes thirteen occurrences of ת\"ק; and above the heads of the living creatures (החיות) there is a firmament that is ת\"ק — that makes fourteen occurrences of ת\"ק in eight firmaments; and the legs of the living creatures are ת\"ק — that makes fifteen. And this is the allusion of ״ורגליהם רגל ישרה״ — so together with the legs of the living creatures at their height, which are ת\"ק, that makes fifteen occurrences of ת\"ק, and the Holy One, blessed be He, is above all. Corresponding to them, the masters of truth established fifteen praises in ישתבח, and in its final one we say לשמך הגדול. And corresponding to them they established fifteen praises in אמת ויציב, all of which praise is bound with the letter vav (וי\"ן) except for the word אמת, which is at the head, and it is the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, in the manner of what is said (Jeremiah 10:10): ״וה' אלהים אמת״. Corresponding to them David said fifteen praises to the Holy One, blessed be He (1 Chronicles 29:11): ״לך ה' הגדולה והגבורה והתפארת והנצח וההוד כי כל בשמים ובארץ לך ה' הממלכה והמתנשא לכל לראש והעשר והכבוד מלפניך ואתה מושל בכל ובידך כח וגבורה ובידך לגדל ולחזק לכל״. Corresponding to them, שיר המעלות (A Song of Ascents) is mentioned fifteen times, and corresponding to them we find fifteen words in the verse (Deuteronomy 26): ״את ה' האמרת״ etc., and corresponding to them, fifteen words in the Priestly Blessing."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:10.594015224Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}